  design, then a written spec. Say the word if you'd rather I collapse this into a short in-chat design instead.

  First question, and it's the one that determines everything downstream.

  [1mThe fork:[0m which surface owns the preferences decides the storage mechanism, the API shape, and whether "session" means
  a browser tab or a process run. These are not cheap to swap later — the persistence layer and the module's public
  interface both change.

  - [1mBrowser (the login webapp)[0m — prefs live in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, the module is a plain script or ES module loaded by
    [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, "persists across sessions" means surviving a tab close. Buys: matches the only real UI here. Costs:
    synchronous string-only storage, no access from the Node side.
  - [1mNode ([38;5;153msrc/[39m)[0m — prefs live in a JSON file on disk (e.g. [38;5;153m~/.config/<app>/preferences.json[39m), CommonJS module, "persists
    across sessions" means surviving a process exit. Buys: real durability, testable without a browser. Costs:
    irrelevant to the login form as it stands.
  - [1mBoth, shared core[0m — one pure preferences module (defaults, validation, merge) with two thin storage adapters. Buys:
    one set of semantics. Costs: roughly double the work and an interface you have to get right up front; premature
    unless you actually need both.

  [1mMy recommendation: browser/[38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m.[0m The login form is the only thing in this repo a user interacts with, and
  "settings persist across sessions" is textbook [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m. The Node side is a greeting stub with no settings to
  speak of. I'd rather build the one real surface well than build an abstraction over a hypothetical second one.
